==Russian==

===Etymology===
{{bor+|ru|cu|врачь}}, from {{der|ru|sla-pro|*vьračь}}. Synchronically equivalent to {{af|ru|врать|-ач|id2=agent noun}}. Originally meant "witch-doctor, enchanter".

===Pronunciation===
* {{ru-IPA}}
* {{audio|ru|Ru-{{PAGENAME}}.ogg|Audio}}
* {{rhymes|ru|at͡ɕ|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{ru-noun+|b|a=an|}}

# {{lb|ru|medicine|formal}} [[therapist]], not [[surgeon]], not [[hospital]] [[nurse]]
#: {{syn|ru|терапе́вт<tr:terapévt, tɛrapévt>}}
# [[doc]], [[vet]], [[medic]], [[physician]], [[doctor]]
#: [[каждый день яблоко одно — врачу оставаться далеко|''Ка́ждый день я́блоко одно́ — '''врачу́''' остава́ться далеко́'']], [[an apple a day keeps the doctor away|An apple a day keeps the doctor away]] (''literally'' every day apple a-one is for the physician to stay away).
#: {{syn|ru|до́ктор|зна́харь|ле́карь|цели́тель}}

====Usage notes====
# A female medical doctor is referred to as {{m|ru|врач}}, or {{m|ru|[[же́нщина]]-врач}}, or, colloquially only, {{m|ru|врачи́ха}}, which is dispreferred.
# The archaic vocative form {{m|ru|вра́чу}} is currently found only in the set phrase:
#: {{uxi|ru|'''вра́чу''', исцели́ся сам|physician, heal thyself}}

